{
  "dom_id": "step-e10603",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Rays and Their Notation",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129501/assets/3e5984eebce9ba2600c980df7910ec2e92b351ea9ae29e8ad30209814deca2dd.png)\n\nConsider the points $R$ and $S$ shown above. Draw the ray $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{RS}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Note the following:\n\n- A ray is a straight object with a specific starting point and extends forever in **one** direction.\n\n- Using diagrams, a ray is shown with an arrow on **one** side.\n\nIn our case, the starting point is $R,$ and the ray extends in the $S$ direction. Therefore, we can draw the ray as follows: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129501/assets/43aaca7cab8f55253aa781896e2ca0070faf6a3c98b98af3950ed307f82f7898.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
